{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 893 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c9c86a722d08566aa556d8db58115f83590776b8bcaeec0bb15a005f545b224c" :edb/db-before-t 892 :edb/db-after-t 893 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534205 50 #inst "2026-10-06T10:37:09.302Z" 13194139534205 true] [17592186157065 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{x+3} + 1 \\geq  0.$" 13194139534205 true] [17592186157065 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{x+3} + 1 \\geq 0$." 13194139534205 false] [17592186157065 1145 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+3}+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+3} & \\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{x+3})}^{3} & \\ge {(-1)}^{3} \\\\ x+3 & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534205 true] [17592186157065 1145 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[3]{x + 3} + 1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x + 3} & \\ge - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[3]{x + 3})^{3} & \\ge (-1)^{3} \\\\ x + 3 & \\ge - 1 \\\\ x & \\ge - 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139534205 false] [17592186157066 1100 "Solve the inequality $3\\sqrt{x-2} +7 \\geq 4$." 13194139534205 false] [17592186157066 1100 "Solve the inequality $3\\sqrt{x-2} +7 \\geq 4.$" 13194139534205 true] [17592186157066 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{x-2}+7 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\nx-2 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\geq 2.$" 13194139534205 true] [17592186157066 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{x - 2} + 7 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge - 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1)$, so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\nx-2 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2\n$$" 13194139534205 false] [17592186157067 1100 "Solve the inequality $7-\\sqrt{x+3} < 5$." 13194139534205 false] [17592186157067 1100 "Solve the inequality $7-\\sqrt{x+3} < 5.$" 13194139534205 true] [17592186157067 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt{x+3} & <5 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x+3} & <-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+3})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ x+3 & >4 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x > 1,$ which can be expressed as $\\left( 1,\\infty \\right).$ The domain of the square root is $x \\geq -3,$ which can be expressed as $\\left[ -3,\\infty \\right).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\left( 1,\\infty \\right) \\cap \\left[ -3,\\infty \\right)  =\\left( 1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in \\left( 1,\\infty\\right),$ which can also be written as $x > 1.$" 13194139534205 true] [17592186157067 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 - \\sqrt{x + 3} & < 5 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x + 3} & < - 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x + 3} & > 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2)$. So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt{x + 3})^{2} & > 2^{2} \\\\ x + 3 & > 4 \\\\ x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x > 1$, which can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nThe domain of the square root is\n\n$$\nx \\geq -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $[MATH: \\left[-3,\\infty \\right).]$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$[MATH: \\left(1,\\infty \\right) \\cap \\left[-3,\\infty \\right) =\\left(1,\\infty\\right)]$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in \\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as $x > 1$." 13194139534205 false] [17592186157373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282112 13194139534205 true] [17592186157373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282138 13194139534205 true] [17592186157373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282143 13194139534205 true] [17592186157373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282148 13194139534205 true] [17592186157373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282176 13194139534205 true] [17592186157374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282119 13194139534205 true] [17592186157374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282126 13194139534205 true] [17592186157374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282154 13194139534205 true] [17592186157374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282159 13194139534205 true] [17592186157374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282165 13194139534205 true] [17592186157375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282131 13194139534205 true] [17592186157375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282171 13194139534205 true] [17592186157375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282183 13194139534205 true] [17592186157375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282190 13194139534205 true] [17592186157832 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534205 true] [17592186157832 1100 "Solve the inequality $-2\\sqrt{3x + 1} + 1 \\ge - 5$." 13194139534205 false] [17592186157832 1100 "Solve the inequality $-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1\\ge -5.$" 13194139534205 true] [17592186157832 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1 & \\ge -5 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3x+1} & \\ge -6 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+1} & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(3).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x+1})}^{2} & \\le {3}^{2} \\\\ 3x+1 & \\le 9 \\\\ 3x & \\le 8 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{8}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}]\\cap [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )=[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{8}{3}.$" 13194139534205 true] [17592186282112 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282113 13194139534205 true] [17592186282112 1089 "q-115031" 13194139534205 true] [17592186282112 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534205 true] [17592186282112 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{2x+1}-1\\le 2.$" 13194139534205 true] [17592186282112 1113 #uuid "887bbbbc-3fb4-56cf-816e-509931191e